findings.body:
handleDragEnd passes (newIndex, oldIndex) to moveItem, but moveItem(from, to) removes the item at from and inserts it at to. Each drag therefore moves the item that sits at the drop position, not the one the user grabbed.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="117-122"}

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="130-145"}

With [A, B, C], dragging A onto C calls moveItem(2, 0). That removes C and inserts it at the front, producing [C, A, B], when it should produce [B, C, A]. The result is also written to the parent through onValuesChange, so the form data is wrong as well as the display.

::callout{type="warning"}
The existing menu actions call moveItem(index, index +/- 1), which is the (from, to) convention. Only the drag path is inverted.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="141-143"}
if (oldIndex !== -1 && newIndex !== -1) {
  moveItem(oldIndex, newIndex);
}

Only the drag handle is disabled for prefix rows. sortableKeys still contains every row, so a regular item can be dropped onto a prefix row's position. handleDragEnd does not check newIndex against the prefix count, so the item lands in a tuple slot.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="147-163"}

Tuple positions have their own schemas (prefixItems[i]). Putting a string where a boolean is expected, or moving a prefix's value out of its slot, breaks the schema contract. The PR description says prefix items remain fixed in position, and this does not hold for drop targets.

::callout{type="warning"}
If the prefix rows are left out of the SortableContext items, dnd-kit will not register them as drop targets. Hovering over them then yields no over, and the drop is a no-op. That keeps the visual displacement consistent with the result.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="147-148"}
// Only regular (non-prefix) rows participate in sorting.
const sortableKeys = localKeyedValues
  .slice(prefixItemsCount)
  .map((item) => item.key);

The PR says it adds "Behavioral tests for move up/down menu options" and a "Test verifying prefix items cannot be moved." The diff contains only four snapshot renders in the array describe block. Nothing clicks the menu, performs a drag, calls moveItem, or calls handleDragEnd.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form.snapshot.test.tsx" lines="489-551"}

This gap is why the reversed moveItem call above was not caught. A snapshot shows that a handle exists in the DOM. It does not show that the handle does anything. The Human Review Checklist items for the drag interaction are also still unchecked, so the drag behavior has not been exercised by the author either.

::callout{type="warning"}
Before merge, either add the behavioral tests the description promises or correct the description.

The menu shows Move up when canMove && !first, where first means index === 0. For a tuple with two prefix items, the first regular row is at index 2, so Move up is shown and calls moveItem(2, 1). That moves the regular item into the prefix slot at index 1.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-item.tsx" lines="111-117"}

This logic predates the PR, but the PR's stated goal is that prefix items stay fixed, and the new drag-and-drop shares the same moveItem path. Fixing the drop targets without fixing this leaves the same breach in place.

::callout{type="info"}
The base file had the same canMove && !first check, so this is not a regression. It becomes more important once drag is available.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-item.tsx" lines="112-114"}
{canMove && !first && (
  <DropdownMenuItem onClick={moveUp}>Move up</DropdownMenuItem>
)}

prefixItemsCount is computed at lines 45-47 but never read; the only index check uses getCanMoveForIndex. The comment above sortableKeys says it gets the keys of items that can be dragged (non-prefix items), but the code maps over all rows, including prefix rows. The comment and the variable both suggest a filter that does not exist. Either use the variable, as suggested in the drop-target finding, or remove it.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="45-47"}

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="147-148"}

The rendered DndDescribedBy-N and DndLiveRegion-N IDs come from dnd-kit's module-level counter. Each snapshot's expected IDs therefore depend on which array tests ran earlier in the same file, and adding or reordering a test shifts every later snapshot. The with enum items snapshot also does not exercise the drag path at all. It renders the multi-select popover with no handles, so its name suggests coverage it does not provide.

::callout{type="info"}
Consider rendering with a stable id prop on DndContext (for example per-test IDs), or asserting on the handle's attributes rather than the global IDs. Rename the enum test to reflect what it covers, or drop it from this PR.
